[
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"110087\">Kim Morrison</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/583341-Model-comparisons-for-Lean/topic/LeanEval/near/598960101\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>computable</code> just means \"do not not attempt to produce compiled code\", i.e. the negation of <code>noncompuatble</code>, which means \"do not attempt to produce compiled code\".</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I am amazed that this works:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Data</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Defs</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Q</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">partOfVertex</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Classical</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">choose</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Q</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Note: no <code>noncomputable</code> in front of <code>partOfVertex</code> even though it uses <code>Classical.choose</code>. And this is not a bug? </p>\n<p>See <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/41446\">#41446</a> which adds about 80 missing <code>noncomputable</code>s (preparing for a change in definition to <code>Set</code>, which exposes all of these).</p>",
        "id": 608743229,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1783438113
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If you define <code>Set</code> as a function to <code>Prop</code>, then <code>partOfVertex</code> is also a function to <code>Prop</code>, so <code>partOfVertex</code> is really (a function returning) a type from the compiler's point of view. Types are computationally irrelevant. They have no form at runtime. So there is nothing for the compiler to do.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, a structure containing a function to <code>Prop</code> is a structure. The rule is that a structure always has a form at runtime, and the runtime representation consists of all of the fields which are computationally relevant. So if you wrap <code>Set</code> in a one-field structure, then you get something at runtime that looks the same as <code>Unit</code> (or any other structure with no fields), because after removing all computationally irrelevant data you get the same in both cases.</p>\n<p>If you run</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Data</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Defs</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Q</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">structure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MySet</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">set_option</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">trace</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Compiler</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">result</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">true</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">partOfVertex</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Classical</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">choose</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Q</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">partOfVertex'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MySet</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⟨</span><span class=\"n\">partOfVertex</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Q</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"bp\">⟩</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>then you can see that on <code>partOfVertex</code> the compiler just doesn't even run, whereas for <code>partOfVertex'</code> it produces code that basically does nothing.</p>",
        "id": 608745751,
        "sender_full_name": "Julia Markus Himmel",
        "timestamp": 1783438751
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>So, can (and should) all of the <code>noncomputable</code>s in <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/41446\">#41446</a> be removed by moving the classical choice inside the one-field structure, as above? <span aria-label=\"eyes\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f440\" role=\"img\" title=\"eyes\">:eyes:</span></p>",
        "id": 608873578,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Murrills",
        "timestamp": 1783480496
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>(Kind of makes you wonder if <code>Set</code>s should be functions to <code>Bool</code>! <span aria-label=\"upside down\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f643\" role=\"img\" title=\"upside down\">:upside_down:</span>)</p>",
        "id": 608874236,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Murrills",
        "timestamp": 1783480887
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"260921\">Julia Markus Himmel</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/270676-lean4/topic/noncomputable/near/608745751\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>then you can see that on <code>partOfVertex</code> the compiler just doesn't even run, whereas for <code>partOfVertex'</code> it produces code that basically does nothing.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Is this the reason why in your snippet <code>noncomputable</code> is not needed even for <code>partOfVertex'</code>? I would have expected, given your first bit of explanation, that it be needed when wrapping inside a structure.</p>",
        "id": 608964830,
        "sender_full_name": "Filippo A. E. Nuccio",
        "timestamp": 1783511640
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>To the compiler, <code>MySet V</code> looks exactly like <code>Unit</code>, because all of the irrelevant fields are ignored. So the reason why <code>partOfVertex'</code> does not need <code>noncomputable</code> is the same reason why <code>a</code> does not need <code>noncomputable</code> in the following example:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Unit</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⟨⟩</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Unit</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">True</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⟨⟨⟩</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">trivial</span><span class=\"bp\">⟩</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- failed to compile definition, consider marking it as 'noncomputable' because it depends on 'Classical.choose', which is 'noncomputable'</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Unit</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Classical</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">choose</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">foo</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 608965962,
        "sender_full_name": "Julia Markus Himmel",
        "timestamp": 1783512041
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"110038\">Kevin Buzzard</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/270676-lean4/topic/noncomputable/near/608743229\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"110087\">Kim Morrison</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/583341-Model-comparisons-for-Lean/topic/LeanEval/near/598960101\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>computable</code> just means \"do not not attempt to produce compiled code\", i.e. the negation of <code>noncompuatble</code>, which means \"do not attempt to produce compiled code\".</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I am amazed that this works:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Data</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Defs</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Q</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">partOfVertex</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Classical</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">choose</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Q</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Note: no <code>noncomputable</code> in front of <code>partOfVertex</code> even though it uses <code>Classical.choose</code>. And this is not a bug? </p>\n<p>See <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/41446\">#41446</a> which adds about 80 missing <code>noncomputable</code>s (preparing for a change in definition to <code>Set</code>, which exposes all of these).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Ooh are we finally making Set a one field structure?</p>",
        "id": 609599928,
        "sender_full_name": "Wrenna Robson",
        "timestamp": 1783766780
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Well Yael has been working on this, who knows whether it gets merged, but there seems to be some support (but this is off-topic for <a class=\"stream\" data-stream-id=\"270676\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/270676-lean4\">#lean4</a> )</p>",
        "id": 609605627,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1783772849
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"110038\">Kevin Buzzard</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/270676-lean4/topic/noncomputable/near/608743229\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am amazed that this works:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Data</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Defs</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Q</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">partOfVertex</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Classical</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">choose</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Q</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Note: no <code>noncomputable</code> in front of <code>partOfVertex</code> even though it uses <code>Classical.choose</code>. And this is not a bug? </p>\n<p>See <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/41446\">#41446</a> which adds about 80 missing <code>noncomputable</code>s (preparing for a change in definition to <code>Set</code>, which exposes all of these).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>As Julia says, this is not a bug, and it's also not new! This has been the expected behaviour since before I started using Lean! <code>Set</code> has had no computational content for a long time, and by extension structures around sets, eg <code>Submodule</code>s don't either - in fact I reported some bugs in the past when things like this were required to be marked <code>noncomputable</code>. The same thing applies to <code>MySet</code> or a hypothetical future change to <code>Set</code>; none of these should need noncomputable!</p>",
        "id": 609618283,
        "sender_full_name": "Bhavik Mehta",
        "timestamp": 1783786128
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Well according to Yael, if you make Set a one field structure then the 80 things in the PR now need to be noncomputable. But discussion about this can probably go elsewhere, this question is now answered.</p>",
        "id": 609619840,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1783787624
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Julia's example above shows that shouldn't need to be the case!</p>",
        "id": 609619909,
        "sender_full_name": "Bhavik Mehta",
        "timestamp": 1783787698
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>But that requires writing the definition in a different way, which might not be the most natural way of writing the definition. To avoid this, we can use <code>noncomputable</code>. I don't think it is clear which of these options is the better one.</p>",
        "id": 609620654,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1783788207
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>One point against <code>noncomputable</code> is that it communicates by its presence in source that this <em>could in principle</em> be computable, and the obstruction is just some classicality assumption, when this is not really what's going on here. (But this isn't a comprehensive point and doesn't necessarily offset the cost of definitions with high awkwardness.)</p>",
        "id": 609632692,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Murrills",
        "timestamp": 1783798975
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I am happy to turn <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/41446\">#41446</a> into a PR removing <code>noncomputable</code> from the relevant definitions so that I don't need to introduce any more in <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/41506\">#41506</a>, if that's preferred.</p>",
        "id": 609676148,
        "sender_full_name": "Yaël Dillies",
        "timestamp": 1783845532
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Related: <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/pull/14084\">lean4#14084</a></p>",
        "id": 611200037,
        "sender_full_name": "Yuyang Zhao",
        "timestamp": 1784257460
    }
]